nes at I9, I10, I11, and I13. There is a single gap at I12. If Black plays at I12, they will form a connected line of 5 stones (I9 to I13), resulting in an immediate win. White currently has strong potential with a diagonal line of three stones (I8, H9, G10), which could form an "Open 4" if White plays at F11 or J7. However, creating an Open 4 is not an immediate win (it wins on the *next* turn). Since Black has a threat to win *immediately* on their turn, White has no time to attack and is forced to block Black\'s winning move.\n\nReason: Black has a split line of four stones in Column I (I9, I10, I11, gap, I13). Playing I12 connects these into a 5-in-a-row. White must block this location to prevent an immediate loss.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 12}'}
